      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let's start by expressing \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of \\(t\\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{t + 1}{t} = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\nNext, we can express \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of each other. First, solve for \\(\\frac{1}{t}\\) in terms of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):\n\\[ x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\implies \\frac{1}{t} = x - 1 \\]\n\\[ y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\implies \\frac{1}{t} = 1 - y \\]\n\nSince both expressions are equal to \\(\\frac{1}{t}\\), we can set them equal to each other:\n\\[ x - 1 = 1 - y \\]\n\\[ x + y = 2 \\]\n\nThis is the equation of a straight line. Therefore, the points \\((x, y)\\) lie on a line.\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
